problems.description_md:
`bracket(text)` borrows a string slice and returns a new `String`: the text with `[` before it and `]` after it, and nothing else changed. Spaces, line feeds, capital letters and accents all stay exactly as they were, and empty text gives `"[]"`.

The starter has one test, `brackets_a_word`. It passes, but one test checks one input, and a function can be right for `"rust"` and wrong for other text. Add a test for each of these cases, so that a version of `bracket` that gets any of them wrong fails at least one test:

- text with spaces around it, such as `"  a b "`;
- empty text;
- text with a line feed in it, such as `"one\ntwo"`;
- text with a combining accent (`"cafe\u{301}"`, which shows as café) or an emoji.

Each test is a function marked `#[test]` that calls `bracket` once and compares the result with `assert_eq!` (Book ch. 11.1). A `String` compares equal to a string literal, so `assert_eq!(bracket("rust"), "[rust]")` works as written.

