problems.description_md:
`Rect::fits(&self, other)` answers "does `other` fit inside `self`?". It is `true` only when `self` is **strictly** wider *and* strictly taller than `other`. A rectangle of the same width (or the same height) doesn't fit, and `other` is never turned sideways.

`fits` returns a `bool`, so the natural check is `assert!` (Book ch. 11.1, "Checking Results with `assert!`"): `assert!(big.fits(&small))` passes when the call returns `true`, and `assert!(!small.fits(&big))` passes when it returns `false`.

The starter tests one case, a rectangle that fits. Add tests for the cases where `fits` must return `false`:

- the same size (equal width and height);
- wider but shorter;
- taller but narrower.

