problems.description_md:
`tag(text)` turns user input into a tag, or says why it can't:

1. Trim whitespace from both ends. That is any Unicode whitespace: spaces, tabs, line feeds and the wide ideographic space `\u{3000}`.
2. If nothing is left, return `Err(TagError::Empty)`.
3. If more than 8 characters are left, return `Err(TagError::TooLong)`. Characters are counted with `chars()`, so `"東京"` is 2 characters even though it is 6 bytes. A letter followed by a combining accent (`"e\u{301}"`) counts as 2.
4. Otherwise return the text in lowercase, `Ok(text.to_lowercase())`, which lowercases non-ASCII letters too: `"ÉTÉ"` becomes `"été"`.

Design the test cases. For a function like this, good cases come from its rules: one case on each side of every limit, one for each error, and inputs that a careless version would mishandle. Write a test for each of these:

- a plain tag with capitals, such as `"Rust"`;
- text padded with mixed whitespace, such as `"\t Rust\u{3000}\n"`;
- exactly 8 characters (accepted) and 9 characters (`TooLong`);
- a tag of more than 8 bytes but at most 8 characters, such as `"東京タワー"`;
- capitals outside ASCII, such as `"ÉTÉ"`;
- empty text and whitespace-only text (`Empty`).

Compare whole results, `Ok(String)` or `Err(TagError)`: `TagError` derives `PartialEq` and `Debug`, so `assert_eq!(tag(""), Err(TagError::Empty), "…")` works. Give each assertion a message naming its case.

starter.text:
#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]
pub enum TagError {
    Empty,
    TooLong,
}

pub fn tag(text: &str) -> Result<String, TagError> {
    let text = text.trim();
    if text.is_empty() {
        return Err(TagError::Empty);
    }
    if text.chars().count() > 8 {
        return Err(TagError::TooLong);
    }
    Ok(text.to_lowercase())
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn lowercases_a_plain_tag() {
        assert_eq!(
            tag("Rust"),
            Ok(String::from("rust")),
            "a plain tag is lowercased"
        );
    }
}

